WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:05.590
With The Martian out now, let’s take a look
at 15 things you probably didn’t know about

00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:11.450
Ridley Scott’s big-screen adaptation of
Andy Weir’s best-selling book.

00:00:11.450 --> 00:00:15.599
Director Ridley Scott didn’t want to rely
on visual effects for the sandstorm scene,

00:00:15.599 --> 00:00:21.029
and wanted to make it feel real for the actors
and audience, so giant fans were used to fill

00:00:21.029 --> 00:00:23.439
the air with dirt and small stones.

00:00:23.439 --> 00:00:27.939
Dust got everywhere including inside the air
vents in the actors’ helmets, which made

00:00:27.939 --> 00:00:32.520
it tough for them to breathe, so between takes
wardrobe assistants had to help them remove

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:33.890
the helmets.

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:38.469
And the thick dust in the air also meant the
actors could hardly see each other while filming.

00:00:38.469 --> 00:00:43.060
Fortunately, the mini-intercom system installed
inside their helmets allowed them to talk

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:44.879
to each other and Scott throughout.

00:00:44.879 --> 00:00:49.760
According to Kate Mara, who plays astronaut
Beth Johanssen, the cast used the intercom

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:54.440
to swap jokes and tease each other, which
helped them bond quickly on set.

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:58.940
The sandstorm scene took three days to film
on the Martian exterior set that was built

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:06.100
on one of the biggest soundstages in the world,
Stage 6 at Korda Studios, near Budapest.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:11.200
As Matt Damon plays astronaut Mark Watney
who gets stranded by himself on Mars, he spent

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:15.630
most of his time filming alone, without the
rest of the cast, for a total of nearly five

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:16.630
weeks.

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:21.090
In fact, his filming schedule only coincided
with Jessica Chastain and the other actors

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:25.610
playing astronauts for three days in mid-December
2014.

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:30.180
And he filmed again with just Chastain for
a couple more days in February 2015.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:36.470
After plans to film in the Australian Outback
didn’t work out, the filmmakers decided

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:40.290
to shoot many of the Martian sequences on
sets at Korda Studios to give them more control

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:41.390
over the environment.

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:46.869
However, the movie also filmed panoramic views
for 8 days in Jordan’s Wadi Rum desert,

00:01:46.869 --> 00:01:48.110
which doubled for Mars.

00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:52.479
Other movies that have shot in the same area
include David Lean’s Lawrence of Arabia

00:01:52.479 --> 00:01:55.510
and the sci-fi horror The Last Days On Mars.

00:01:55.510 --> 00:02:01.090
Back in the studio, a huge green screen, over
300 foot long and 65 foot high, was assembled

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:04.240
so background plates of Wadi Rum could be
added.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:08.820
And to ensure that shots taken on the stage
matched those from Jordan, the movie’s visual

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:13.780
effects supervisor and cinematographer studied
Wadi Rum’s solar path charts so they knew

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:17.800
the correct lighting direction at all times.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:23.000
4,000 tons of soil and other materials were
used to create the Martian landscape set.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:28.220
It took three types of Hungarian soil mixed
together by hand and machine to get the right

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:30.550
colour to match the Wadi Rum desert.

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:36.360
A nursery with artificial lighting and heating
was also built at the studio to grow Watney’s

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:39.680
potatoes, using half-cut spuds, just like
in the movie.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:46.490
A total of 1,200 potatoes were grown, with
around 8 potatoes per plant.

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:50.400
The Martian’s Rover was modelled on the
Curiosity rover, though the movie’s version

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:51.560
is even bigger.

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:56.930
A team of 22 technicians from the movie crew
and 15 members of the Hungarian Opel Dakar

00:02:56.930 --> 00:03:00.360
team constructed two full-scale Rovers for
filming.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:04.960
But before shooting began in Jordan, the vehicle’s
ability to deal with rough terrain was put

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:07.420
to the test in a Hungarian quarry.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:11.050
While the solar panels on the vehicle’s
exterior make it look as if it runs on solar

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:17.210
energy, which is part of the story; in reality
it had a 2-litre diesel engine.

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:22.020
The design of the Hermes spacecraft was influenced
by the interlocking modules of the International

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:23.160
Space Station.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:27.870
The Hermes set was built on several stages
at Korda Studios, and the craft’s bright

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:33.770
white interior is a nod to director Stanley
Kubrick’s classic scif-fi, 2001: A Space

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:35.900
Odyssey.

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:40.440
To make it look like the actors were floating
around in zero gravity, an enormous winch

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:46.340
system was built and suspended above the roofless
set of the Hermes and the actors were harnessed

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:49.250
to wire rigs at the waist, legs and shoulders.

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:53.220
Although the system was mechanised, members
of the stunt team were still needed to pull

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:54.961
harness ropes to make the actors move vertically.
150 metres of truss, 90 metres of track, 70

00:03:54.961 --> 00:03:55.961
pulleys, and around 400 metres of rope were
used to build the rig.

00:03:55.961 --> 00:03:56.961
To get inspiration for the movie’s spacesuits,
costume designer Janty Yates visited the Johnson

00:03:56.961 --> 00:03:57.961
Space Center and the Jet Propulsion Lab, researched
spacesuit designs with the Curator of Space

00:03:57.961 --> 00:03:58.961
History at the Smithsonian National Air And
Space Museum, and even saw designs for suits

00:03:58.961 --> 00:03:59.961
that are going to be used on missions beyond
2030.

00:03:59.961 --> 00:04:01.704
15 suits were made for the film, and the actors
wore them for 10 to 12 hours a day.

00:04:01.704 --> 00:04:02.704
The surface suits, which were worn when exploring
Mars, were made of neoprene, which is also

00:04:02.704 --> 00:04:03.704
used for wetsuits, and their combined weight
with the helmet was over 30 pounds.

00:04:03.704 --> 00:04:04.704
The movie’s Extra-Vehicular Activity suit
or ‘outer space’ suit, which was worn

00:04:04.704 --> 00:04:05.704
outside the Hermes spacecraft, is a more streamlined
version of a NASA suit used outside the International

00:04:05.704 --> 00:04:06.704
Space Station.

00:04:06.704 --> 00:04:07.704
Underneath the suits, there was a system of
cold water tubes to keep the actors cool,

00:04:07.704 --> 00:04:08.704
and fans inside their life support backpacks
also channelled air through a hose into their

00:04:08.704 --> 00:04:09.704
helmets, which was the same as the cooling
system used in the spacesuits in Interstellar.

00:04:09.704 --> 00:04:10.704
