00:04:10.704 --> 00:04:11.704
click right there!

00:04:11.704 --> 00:04:12.704
To get inspiration for the movie’s spacesuits,
costume designer Janty Yates visited NASA

00:04:12.704 --> 00:04:14.620
facilities, researched spacesuit designs with
the Curator of Space History at the Smithsonian

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:18.840
National Air And Space Museum, and even saw
designs for suits that are going to be used

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:20.630
on missions beyond 2030.

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:25.320
15 suits were made for the film, and the actors
wore them for around 10 hours a day.

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:29.780
The surface suits worn for exploring Mars
in the film are made of neoprene, which is

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:31.750
also used for wetsuits.

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:36.470
The movie’s Extravehicular Activity suit
or ‘outer space’ suit, which was worn

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:41.580
outside the Hermes, is a more streamlined,
less bulky version of the Extravehicular Mobility

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:46.450
Units worn by astronauts on spacewalks at
the International Space Station.

00:04:46.450 --> 00:04:50.850
Underneath the suits, there was a system of
cold water tubes to keep the actors cool,

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:55.390
and fans inside their life support backpacks
also channelled air through a hose into their

00:04:55.390 --> 00:05:00.460
helmets, which was the same as the cooling
system used in Interstellar’s spacesuits.

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:04.420
If you want to check out some more cool facts
