00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:07.120
click right there!

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:11.870
To get good, clear close-ups of the actors’
faces, the helmets made for the movie had

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:14.490
to be bigger than the ones worn by real astronauts.

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:19.760
Also, each helmet’s visor had to be flawless
and ultra-polished to prevent the kind of

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:24.820
glare that makes it very difficult to capture
the faces of real astronauts on camera when

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:26.000
they’re in space.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:30.440
Again, to keep the actors’ faces visible
while filming, the helmets also had several

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:35.680
lights in them which were operated separately
by a small remote.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:38.980
The filmmakers worked closely with NASA from
script to shoot.

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:43.360
For example, they were allowed to film rocket
launches at Cape Canaveral, including the

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:48.340
lift-off of the Orion, a spacecraft designed
to eventually enable human exploration of

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:49.340
Mars.

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:54.990
In fact, on board the Orion during its maiden
voyage in December 2014 was the title page

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:59.690
of The Martian script, which included a sketch
of astronaut Mark Watney by director Ridley

00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:00.690
Scott!

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:06.120
To prepare for her role as Commander Lewis,
leader of the Mars mission, Jessica Chastain

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:11.110
spent a number of days at NASA’s Jet Propulsion
Laboratory and the Johnson Space Center and

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:13.630
got to go inside a Mars Ascent Vehicle.

00:06:13.630 --> 00:06:18.560
Chastain also did background reading on astronauts
including Sally Ride, and spent time with

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:23.330
astronaut and chemist Tracy Caldwell Dyson
who shared her experience of both the technical

00:06:23.330 --> 00:06:25.770
and human side of life in space.

00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:31.210
On top of that, via a virtual reality headset,
Chastain experienced panoramic images of Mars

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:34.790
taken by the Curiosity rover.

00:06:34.790 --> 00:06:39.000
Production designer Arthur Max found much
of his inspiration for the movie’s NASA

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:43.770
sets on a tour of the Johnson Space Center,
led by Dr James Green, who, as well as being

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:49.490
Director of NASA’s Planetary Science Division,
also happens to be a huge fan of Alien!

00:06:49.490 --> 00:06:54.100
During the tour, Max spoke with lots of specialists
and took hundreds of photos for reference.

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:59.240
He also inspected the old Apollo mission control
centre and the current Centre, and incorporated

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:05.610
aspects of those into his own vision of NASA’s
next control room in The Martian.

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:09.770
During filming, the monitors in the movie’s
Mission Control Room displayed a mixture of

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:13.620
graphics, high-res satellite imagery and video
footage from NASA.

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:18.340
Ridley Scott preferred this to using green
screen and adding the images in post-production

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:23.300
as it allowed him to use the images on the
screens as light sources and it gave the actors

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:25.090
something real to react to.

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:28.660
By the way, director Colin Trevorrow did something
similar in Jurassic World.

00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:32.530
And if you’d like to find out more things
you didn’t know about that movie, click

00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:35.230
right there.

00:07:35.230 --> 00:07:40.040
Filming in Budapest also took place at The
Whale, a cultural and commercial centre located

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:42.040
along the Danube River.

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:45.550
The state-of-the-art building was used for
scenes set in the offices of NASA director

00:07:45.550 --> 00:07:50.620
Teddy Sanders and media relations director
Annie Montrose, played by Jeff Daniels and

00:07:50.620 --> 00:07:55.140
Kristen Wiig, and it was also used for scenes
involving conference rooms, a break room and

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:57.080
coffee shop, and a main entrance.

00:07:57.080 --> 00:08:01.980
To make the maximum use of the building’s
open spaces, fake concrete walls were placed

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:07.740
on wheels and used to quickly create and then
change the layout of offices, as needed for

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:09.010
filming.

00:08:09.010 --> 00:08:13.860
Kate Mara was already such a huge fan of Ridley
Scott before she was even cast in The Martian

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:18.220
that she had named one of her dogs Lucius,
after the Emperor’s nephew in the Oscar-winning

00:08:18.220 --> 00:08:19.870
historical drama, Gladiator.

